<italic>CCTACGGGAGGCAGCAG</italic>
</td><td char="." align="char">
<sup>7</sup>
</td></tr><tr><td align="left"/><td align="left">16S_SW-R</td><td align="left">
<italic>ATTACCGCGGCTGCTGG</italic>
</td><td align="left"/></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>1. Monteiro, J., Widen, R. H., Pignatari, A. C. C., Kubasek, C. &#x00026; Silbert, S. Rapid detection of carbapenemase genes by multiplex real-time PCR. <italic>Journal of Antimicrobial Chemotherapy</italic><bold>67</bold>, 906&#x02013;909 (2012).</p><p>2. Guzman-Otazo, J. et al. Diarrheal bacterial pathogens and multi-resistant enterobacteria in the Choqueyapu River in La Paz, Bolivia. <italic>PLoS One</italic><bold>14</bold>, 1&#x02013;20 (2019).</p><p>3. Flach, C. F., Hutinel, M., Razavi, M., &#x000c5;hr&#x000e9;n, C. &#x00026; Larsson, D. G. J. Monitoring of hospital sewage shows both promise and limitations as an early-warning system for carbapenemase-producing Enterobacterales in a low-prevalence setting. <italic>Water Res</italic><bold>200</bold>, (2021).</p><p>4. Xi, C. et al. Prevalence of antibiotic resistance in drinking water treatment and distribution systems. <italic>Appl Environ Microbiol</italic><bold>75</bold>, 5714&#x02013;5718 (2009).</p><p>5. Alexander, J., Bollmann, A., Seitz, W. &#x00026; Schwartz, T. Microbiological characterization of aquatic microbiomes targeting taxonomical marker genes and antibiotic resistance genes of opportunistic bacteria. <italic>Sci Total Environ</italic><bold>512&#x02013;513</bold>, 316&#x02013;325 (2015).</p><p>6. Hembach, N. et al. Occurrence of the mcr-1 colistin resistance gene and other clinically relevant antibiotic resistance genes in microbial populations at different municipal wastewater treatment plants in Germany. <italic>Front Microbiol</italic><bold>8</bold>, (2017).</p><p>7. Muyzer, G., de Waal, E. C. &#x00026; Uitterlinden, A. G. Rapid Lipid Biomarker Analysis for Quantitative Assessment of Microbial Community Composition and Activity. <italic>Applied and Environmental Microbiology Microbiology</italic><bold>59</bold>, 695&#x02013;700 (1993).</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap>
</p><p id="Par22">qPCR was performed on an AriaMx Agilent (AriaMx Real-time PCR System, Agilent) using Syber Green, and according to MIQE guidelines<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR15">15</xref></sup>. Each qPCR run was performed in triplicate in a final volume of 10&#x000a0;&#x000b5;l which contained 5&#x000a0;&#x000b5;l of Power SYBR&#x02122; Green PCR Master mix (Applied Biosystems, Carlsbad, CA), 2&#x000a0;&#x000b5;l of primers (5pmol/&#x000b5;l) and 3 &#x000b5;L (2ng for 16&#x000a0;S gene and 5ng for the ARG genes) of DNA. Reactions were conducted using the Agilent Aria 1.8 system with the following thermal profile: AmpliTaqGold<sup>&#x000ae;</sup> Polymerase activation at 95&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C for 10&#x000a0;min, followed by 40 cycles of denaturation at 95&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C for 15&#x000a0;s. The annealing/extension temperatures were set as follows: <italic>blaNDM</italic> at 58&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C, <italic>blaOXA-48</italic> at 55&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C, <italic>blaKPC</italic> at 58&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C, <italic>blaIMP</italic> at 56&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C, <italic>blaVIM</italic> at 58&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C, <italic>blaCTX</italic> at 57&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C, <italic>mcr-1</italic> at 58&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C, 16&#x000a0;S at 60&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C, and <italic>blaSHV</italic> at 64&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C for 1&#x000a0;min. A standard curve was established through a 10-fold serial dilution of control bacteria hosting the target genes. To determine the specificity of the amplification, a melting curve was recorded by increasing the temperature from 60&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C to 95&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C. Data analysis was performed using Agilent Aria 1.8 software.</p></sec><sec id="Sec7"><title>Statistical analysis</title><p id="Par23">Principal Component Analysis (PCA) of physicochemical parameters was conducted utilizing the FactoMiner package in the RStudio software (version 4.3.3). Figures were generated employing the ggplot2, tidyr, and tidyverse packages. Alpha diversity (index&#x02009;=&#x02009;Shannon), Beta diversity analysis (method&#x02009;=&#x02009;Bray), Permutational Multivariate Analysis of Variance (PERMANOVA), and Analysis of Similarities (ANOSIM) tests were executed through the vegan package within RStudio<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref></sup>. The abundance data was first transformed using a log transformation (log1p), and then it was subjected to min-max scaling to normalize the values between 0 and 1. The outcomes of these analyses were then visually represented using the ggplot2 package.</p><p id="Par24">Canonical Correspondence Analysis (CCA) was performed using the vegan package in R. This analysis enabled the exploration of relationships between physicochemical parameters and antibiotic resistance gene (ARG) composition in the dataset. Spearman correlation analysis among Mobile Genetic Elements (MGE), Antibiotic Resistance Genes (ARG), microbiome and species was executed utilizing the ggplot2 and dplyr libraries in R. The resistome and microbiome co-occurrence network analysis was executed utilizing the igraph, readr, ggraph, dplyr, and ggforce libraries. This analytical approach involved establishing a correlation threshold of 0.6, enabling the identification of strong associations among the data points.</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec8" sec-type="results"><title>Results</title><sec id="Sec9"><title>Physicochemical parameters</title><p id="Par25">Sampling of wastewater was conducted in February (Feb) and July (Jul) 2020 with the purpose of evaluating seasonal fluctuations in physicochemical parameters at designated stations (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab3" ref-type="table">3</xref>). The choice of February and July is dictated by the fact that the region is characterized by a semi-arid to sub-desert climate; most of the rainfall is limited to the period between November and March, while the dry season lasts from May to October<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR17">17</xref></sup>. Throughout both sampling periods, effluent temperatures were maintained below 30&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C. In February, temperatures remained consistently within the range of 18&#x02013;20&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C, showing negligible disparities between influent (IF) and effluent (EF) values. Conversely, in July, a slight rise in temperature was observed, ranging from 19 to 26&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C. pH levels fluctuated between 7 and 8 during February and 6&#x02013;8 in July. Notably, all recorded values remained below 8.5, indicating compliance with acceptable thresholds for direct discharge into the receiving environment.</p><p id="Par26">
