</p><p id="Par27">Significant differences in conductivity were observed between the two sampling periods, with certain stations surpassing specified limits. Of particular concern was the ANZA station, which recorded notably high values exceeding the prescribed thresholds. This could potentially be explained by the fact that the ANZA station receives salt-laden waters originating from fish processing industries located in its area. Dissolved oxygen levels were higher in July, accompanied by elevated turbidity in influents. COD indicated a reduction post-treatment, although certain effluents slightly exceeded discharge limits. While influent COD levels were notably high, they decreased following treatment. However, select effluents marginally surpassed the direct discharge threshold of 500&#x000a0;mg/L. Calcium, chlorides, magnesium, water hardness, and carbonate and bicarbonate concentrations were relatively moderate to low based on the sampled data from both periods. Analysis of heavy metals revealed trace amounts of antimony not exceeding 0.2&#x000a0;mg/L. The origin of antimony is primarily due to anthropogenic inputs, as it is largely absent from the geological strata. Antibiotic resistance genes occur naturally in various environments in low abundance. However, studies have shown that their abundance increase in the presence of pollutants such as heavy metals, crude oil and sewage<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR18">18</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR19">19</xref></sup>.</p><p id="Par28">Principal Component Analysis revealed correlations among various parameters, indicating correlation between temperature, chlorides, salinity, and calcium, alongside TAC, COD, turbidity, and conductivity. Additionally, pH demonstrated positive correlations with dissolved oxygen, total hardness, and magnesium content. These correlations accounted for 34.3% and 22.6% of the total variance, respectively (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>A). Furthermore, analyzing temporal shifts between February and July showed significant disparities due to seasonal variables such as inputs of rain water experienced only in February and/or touristic activities that are more common in July (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>B).</p><p id="Par29">
<fig id="Fig2"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Principal Component Analysis of physicochemical parameters.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_76773_Fig2_HTML" id="d33e1681"/></fig>
</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec10"><title>Resistome analysis</title><p id="Par30">A sum of 105,853,819 reads were recorded, comprising 1,373,434 reads related to Antibiotic Resistance Genes and 88,642,264 reads that were successfully aligned with the 16&#x000a0;S reference. A cumulative count of 31 distinct antibiotic drug classes and 223 antibiotic resistance genes were documented. The relative abundance of each drug class and ARG is expressed as copies per 1&#x000a0;million 16&#x000a0;S equivalent cells<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR14">14</xref></sup>. Among the 31 drug classes, the most abundant three drug classes are Aminoglycosides, Lincosamide, Macrolide and Streptogramins (MLS), in addition to Macrolide antibiotics (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig3" ref-type="fig">3</xref>). The corresponding ARGs linked with these drug classes consistently appear in all samples. The relative abundances of these ARGs encompass a range from 535 to 726,222 copies per 1&#x000a0;million 16&#x000a0;S equivalent cells.</p><p id="Par31">
<fig id="Fig3"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>The 10 most abundant drug classes.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_76773_Fig3_HTML" id="d33e1702"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par32">In the February timeframe, when analyzing station data, the input of TIZNIT station stands out with a notable count of 726,222 copies of aminoglycoside antibiotics. Following closely is the ANZA input station, which records 559,743 copies of macrolide antibiotics. Meanwhile, the input of M&#x02019;ZAR station and AOURIR station exhibit relatively lower abundance in drug classes, with 47,504 copies of MLS and 61,379 copies of macrolide antibiotics, respectively. During the same period, in terms of plant effluents, the ANZA and DRARGA plants displayed the most significant levels of abundance. The ANZA plant exhibited 305,153 copies of macrolide antibiotics, while the DRARGA plant showcased 283,028 copies of aminoglycoside antibiotics. In contrast, the M&#x02019;ZAR and AOURIR stations demonstrated a comparatively lower relative abundance, with 10,226 copies and 87,475 copies of aminoglycoside antibiotics, respectively. In the month of July, the DRARGA and M&#x02019;ZAR inlets registered the most notable levels of abundance, with 386,153 and 314,768 copies of aminoglycoside antibiotics, respectively. Conversely, the ANZA and TIZNIT locations documented the lowest figures, displaying 70,102 and 172,359 copies of aminoglycoside antibiotics, respectively. In terms of station outputs, significant figures were noted at the M&#x02019;ZAR and TIZNIT stations, with 301,468 copies of aminoglycoside antibiotics and 134,790 copies of lincosamide antibiotics recorded, respectively.</p><p id="Par33">In contrast, the AOURIR and ANZA stations recorded significantly lower levels of aminoglycoside antibiotics, with 35,328 copies at AOURIR and 85,754 copies at ANZA. A related study by Lira et al. using metagenomic analysis of an urban resistome before and after wastewater treatment, detected a substantial presence of macrolide resistance genes<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR20">20</xref></sup>. Similarly, another study on the community composition and resistome in a cold climate wastewater treatment plant identified high levels of genes linked to tetracycline resistance, peptide antibiotics, and macrolides<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR21">21</xref></sup>. Additionally, Rodriguez et al.&#x02018;s analysis of urban wastewater resistome and mobilome highlighted the prevalence of genes conferring resistance to macrolide-lincosamide-streptogramin, &#x003b2;-lactams, and multidrug resistance<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR22">22</xref></sup>. At SIDI TOUAL and ANZA beaches, we observed aminoglycoside-related genes at 2,237 and 12,490 copies, respectively, and macrolide-related genes at 860 and 489 copies.</p><p id="Par34">Among the most abundant ARGs are <italic>aadA</italic> (aminoglycoside adenylyltransferase), <italic>ere</italic> (macrolide esterases), <italic>erm</italic> (erythromycin resistance methylase), <italic>Lnu</italic> (lincosamide nucleotidyltransferase), <italic>mph</italic> (macrolide phosphotransferase), and <italic>tet</italic> (tetracycline-resistant ribosomal protection protein) (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig4" ref-type="fig">4</xref>A). In February, prominent resistance genes at influent stations comprised <italic>erm(B)</italic> with 346,472 copies at ANZA WWTP, <italic>mph(E)</italic> at the same station with 510,895 copies, <italic>strB/aph6</italic> at TIZINIT with 154,573 copies, and <italic>aadA13</italic> also at the same station. In July, the prevailing genes remained <italic>mph(E)</italic> at DRAGRA with 162,811 copies, <italic>aadS</italic> at the same station with 150,470 copies, <italic>lnu(F)/lin(F)</italic> at AOURIR with 95,659 copies, and <italic>strB/aph6</italic> at DRARGA with 81,777 copies. In the case of effluents, during the February period, the most abundant resistance genes consisted of <italic>mph(E)</italic> with 282,551 copies at ANZA, <italic>strB/aph6</italic> with 72,840 copies at AIT BAHA, and <italic>lnu(F)/lin(F)</italic> and <italic>lnu(G)/lin(G)</italic> with 80,774 and 70,823 copies at TIZNIT and AIT BAHA, respectively. Come July, the prevailing genes included <italic>lnu(G)/lin(G)</italic> with 99,197 copies at L&#x02019;MZAR, <italic>erm(F)</italic> with 84,355 copies at TIZNIT, and <italic>mph(E)</italic> with 48,629 copies at L&#x02019;MZAR. In the SIDI TOUAL and ANZA beaches, the most abundant genes are integrase with 1,032 copies (SIDI TOUAL), <italic>strB/aph6</italic> with 12,050 copies (ANZA), <italic>mph(E)</italic> with 585 copies (SIDI TOUAL), and <italic>erm(F)</italic> with 831 copies (ANZA).</p><p id="Par35">
<fig id="Fig4"><label>Fig. 4</label><caption><p><bold>A</bold>. Plot abundance of the 20 most abundant ARG, <bold>B</bold>. Plot abundance of other resistance genes, <bold>C</bold>. Alpha diversity using Shannon index, <bold>D</bold>. Beta diversity using Bray-Curtis method.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_76773_Fig4_HTML" id="d33e1821"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par36">The most predominant resistance genes in this investigation include <italic>mph(E)</italic>, <italic>erm(B)</italic>, <italic>aadA</italic>, and <italic>strB/aph6</italic>. The <italic>mph(E)</italic> resistance gene is typically associated with the encoding of a macrolide phosphotransferase enzyme, conferring resistance to macrolide antibiotics like erythromycin. Notably, this gene displayed a significant presence in the effluent originating from wastewater treatment plants near the Tama River and Tokyo<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR23">23</xref></sup>. Its prevalence was universal, as it was detected in every sample during genomic analysis of sewage collected from 101 countries, providing a global perspective on antimicrobial resistance<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR24">24</xref></sup>. Conversely, the <italic>erm(B)</italic> resistance gene encodes a ribosomal methyltransferase enzyme, conferring resistance to macrolide, lincosamide, and streptogramin B antibiotics. Notably, this gene stood out as the primary antimicrobial resistance gene identified in the influent of wastewater treatment plants<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR25">25</xref></sup>. Furthermore, the <italic>erm(B)</italic> gene was quantified at significantly higher levels in the effluents of various municipal wastewater treatment plants across Germany, as highlighted in Norman&#x02019;s study<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR26">26</xref></sup>.</p><p id="Par37">Additionally, the <italic>strB/aph6</italic> gene, which often encodes a bifunctional enzyme in bacteria, possessing two distinct activities&#x02014;streptomycin 3&#x02019;&#x02019;-adenylyltransferase (strB) and kanamycin 6&#x02019;&#x02019;-adenylyltransferase (aph6) was identified in an urban wastewater treatment plant<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR27">27</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR28">28</xref></sup>.</p><p id="Par38">For additional antimicrobial resistance genes quantified using qPCR, the copy count exhibited a marked increase in July 2020 as opposed to February. Among the prevalent genes detected in the majority of samples, the top four in terms of abundance and commonality are <italic>blaSHV</italic>, <italic>blaCTX</italic>, <italic>blaOXA-48</italic>, and <italic>blaVIM</italic> (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig4" ref-type="fig">4</xref>B). The <italic>blaSHV</italic> and <italic>blaCTX</italic> genes stood out as the most prevalent, with <italic>blaSHV</italic> being the predominant one. These findings were consistently observed in numerous wastewater treatment plants worldwide<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR29">29</xref></sup>. The number of copies fluctuates between 5 and 6000 copies. Regarding the <italic>bla</italic><italic>IMP</italic> gene, its detection is primarily concentrated at the AOURIR station outlet during both time periods, exhibiting a copy number of 24,474 in the month of July. Ana&#x02019;s study shed light on its prevalence, notably showing that its relative abundance increased significantly with the size of the wastewater treatment plant, emphasizing its environmental impact<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR30">30</xref></sup>. The <italic>blaKPC</italic> gene primarily manifests during the July timeframe, observable at the entrances and exits of M&#x02019;ZAR and ANZA, as well as in the influents of AOURIR and TIZNIT. Concerning <italic>mcr-1</italic>, its predominant presence is observed primarily at the ANZA exit in February, registering 128 copies. Additionally, it is detected at the entrance of TZINIT during both periods, with counts of 389 and 146 copies respectively. Notably, <italic>mcr-1</italic> is also identified at the AIT BAHA station, with 118 copies detected at both the entrance and exit points, as well as at the DRARGA entrance in July, exhibiting a count of 441 copies.</p><p id="Par39">The detection of <italic>mcr-1</italic> positive bacteria in influent wastewater samples from WWTPs in Germany, and in some cases, in effluent samples after conventional treatment, highlights the potential for these resistant bacteria to enter the aquatic environment<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR26">26</xref></sup>.</p><p id="Par40">The analysis of alpha diversity using the Shannon index revealed interesting insights into the richness and evenness of ARGs (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig4" ref-type="fig">4</xref>C). The calculated Shannon index values showed that ARG richness decreases after the treatment of wastewater, with beach samples presenting the lowest index. In assessing beta diversity, we employed the Bray-Curtis dissimilarity metric (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig4" ref-type="fig">4</xref>D). The findings indicated that the wastewater samples (both raw and treated) exhibited lower dissimilarity to each other compared to the beach samples.</p><p id="Par41">When comparing all five groups (Raw wastewater of February: R-Feb, Raw wastewater of July: R-Jul, Treated wastewater of February: T-Feb, Treated wastewater of July: T-Jul, Samples of beaches: B) using PERMANOVA, a significant difference was observed with an F-statistic of 2.3779 and a p-value of 0.003 (**), indicating meaningful distinctions among the groups. However, when specifically examining the four groups (R-Feb, R-Jul, T-Feb, and T-Jul) through PERMANOVA, the analysis yielded an R-statistic of 1.3652 and p-value (&#x0003e;&#x02009;0.05), suggesting no significant differences within these groups. Additionally, ANOSIM testing produced an R-statistic of 0.04707 and p-value (&#x0003e;&#x02009;0.05), implying insignificant differences, potentially attributed to random variation.</p></sec><sec id="Sec11"><title>Microbiome analysis</title><p id="Par42">The 16&#x000a0;S primer pool enables the characterization of the microbiome with a resolution that extends to the species level. The Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>A illustrates the 20 species with the highest levels of abundance in the collected samples (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>A). Predominantly featured among these species are <italic>Acetobacterium sp</italic>, <italic>Achromobacter sp</italic>, <italic>Acidovorax sp</italic>, and <italic>Acinetobacter sp</italic>. The ANZA station exhibits the highest diversity in terms of both entry and exit during the month of February. In the inflow of DRARGRA and TIZINIT stations in July, <italic>Acetobacterium sp</italic> is primarily observed at relative abundance of 0.05%. <italic>Achromobacter sp</italic> is predominantly detected at the outlet of the TIZNIT station in both July and February, with relative abundance of 0.08% and 0.04%, respectively. <italic>Acidovorax sp</italic> is predominantly observed in the effluents of AOURIR during both February and July, accounting for 0.2% of the microbial community in February and 0.11% in July. It is also detected in the effluent of TIZNIT in July, constituting 0.1% of the microbial community. <italic>Acinetobacter sp</italic> is primarily detected in the inflow of ANZA during February, making up 1.18% of the microbial community. It is also observed in the effluent of the same station during the same period, accounting for 0.8% of the microbial community.</p><p id="Par43">
<fig id="Fig5"><label>Fig. 5</label><caption><p><bold>A</bold>. Plot abundance of the 20 most abundant species, <bold>B</bold>. Plot abundance of other species, <bold>C</bold>. Alpha diversity using Shannon index, <bold>D</bold>. Beta diversity using Bray-Curtis method.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_76773_Fig5_HTML" id="d33e2000"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par44">A total of twenty-one species, which possess the capacity to spread resistance within the environment and potentially transmit it to humans, including the ESKAPE pathogens, have been identified. Out of these, 18 species have been observed (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>B). The species predominantly detected across all samples include <italic>Streptococcus salivarius</italic>, <italic>Escherichia coli</italic>, <italic>Klebsiella pneumoniae</italic>, and <italic>Citrobacter freundii</italic>. Notably, <italic>Streptococcus salivarius</italic> was observed at the ANZA station in February, accounting for 7% at the outlet and 5% at the inlet. <italic>Escherichia coli</italic> was primarily identified at the same station, comprising 13% at the inlet and 4% at the outlet. <italic>E. coli</italic>, has emerged as a major concern due to its increasing antibiotic resistance. This resistance not only hampers the effectiveness of conventional therapies but also elevates the risk of prolonged illnesses, healthcare-associated infections, and broader community dissemination<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR31">31</xref></sup>. <italic>Klebsiella pneumoniae</italic> was primarily detected at the M&#x02019;ZAR station inlet in July, the DRARGA inlet in February, and the ANZA outlet during the same period, with a relative abundance of 2%. Despite this, the pathogenic bacteria were eliminated after treatment at both MZAR in July and Drarga in February. In contrast, at the ANZA site, treatment was less effective, as the bacteria persisted in the February effluent and even showed an increase post-treatment during this period. <italic>Klebsiella pneumoniae</italic> is among the most prevalent gram-negative strains commonly found in hospital wastewater, heightening concerns regarding its role in the development and spread of antibiotic resistance genes<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR32">32</xref></sup>. Moreover, <italic>Klebsiella pneumoniae</italic> is considered a key trafficker of drug resistance genes from environmental to clinically important bacteria<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR33">33</xref></sup>.The persistence of such bacteria, especially in effluents, underscores the challenges in controlling antibiotic-resistant pathogens and the need for more effective wastewater treatment processes.</p><p id="Par45"><italic>Citrobacter freundii</italic> was detected at the M&#x02019;ZAR inlet in July, constituting 2.6%, but was eliminated after treatment. At the ANZA outlet in February, it comprised 1.4%, yet it was not present during the July period. In February, the ANZA inlet and outlet exhibited the highest prevalence of these species, whereas in July, the M&#x02019;ZAR and DRARGA inlets displayed the highest occurrence. Regarding the other bacteria mentioned in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>B, they are nearly absent.</p><p id="Par46">The Shannon index values indicated that treated wastewater in July exhibited higher diversity compared to both raw wastewater and treated wastewater in February (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>C). Using the Bray-Curtis method, the analysis revealed less dissimilarity between raw and treated wastewater samples, while beach samples demonstrated marked dissimilarity, highlighting the distinct microbial communities present in the natural environment (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>D). A PERMANOVA test comparing all five groups revealed a significant difference, with an F-statistic of 2.411 and a p-value of 0.001 (*), indicating meaningful distinctions across these groups. Narrowing the focus to four groups (R-Feb, R-Jul, T-Feb, and T-Jul), the PERMANOVA analysis showed a significant variation with an R-statistic of 1.7331 and a p-value of 0.01, underscoring the differences between raw and treated wastewater across months. Further statistical analysis using ANOSIM yielded an R-statistic of 0.1534 and a p-value of 0.017, confirming significant differences between the groups. The observed variations are linked to the nature of the influent, whether it is domestic (urban or rural) or industrial, as well as the wastewater treatment process employed. Additionally, the presence of pathogenic bacteria from the ESKAPE group in the effluent, such as at the ANZA WWTP and ANZA Beach, raises significant concerns. These findings indicate that while treatment reduces the overall bacterial load, the microbial community composition changes considerably with seasonal fluctuations and the specific treatment processes used, particularly when compared to natural environments like beach samples.</p><sec id="Sec12"><title>Efficiency of WWTPs</title><p id="Par47">The examined wastewater treatment facilities employ various treatment methods. The M&#x02019;ZAR plant utilizes anaerobic decantation, whereas the AOURIR plant employs activated sludge. The ANZA plant employs lamellar decantation, while both the TIZNIT and AIT BAHA plants resort to natural lagooning. The final DRAGARA plant utilizes infiltration and percolation over sand. The efficacy of the employed treatments was assessed at all stations during both time periods. The formula employed for evaluation was ((influent_concentration - effluent_concentration) / influent_concentration) * 100. This assessment pertains to the purification efficiency of four constituents: ARGs, ORGs (other resistance genes), the microbiome, and the species accountable for the propagation of resistance (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig6" ref-type="fig">6</xref>).</p><p id="Par48">
<fig id="Fig6"><label>Fig. 6</label><caption><p>Removal Efficiency of WWTPs.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_76773_Fig6_HTML" id="d33e2081"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par49">It is important to highlight that there is a variation in treatment efficacy when comparing the two periods. The optimal approach for ARG elimination was observed at the AOURIR facility in July, achieving an 86% reduction, closely trailed by TIZNIT in February at 81%. Conversely, AOURIR in February and ANZA in July exhibited subpar efficacy. In terms of ORG elimination, the TIZNIT treatment exhibited exceptional performance with a consistent 98% reduction in both periods. Notably, the AOURIR station displayed reduced effectiveness due to the prevalence of the <italic>blaIMP</italic> gene in its effluents. Furthermore, the AIT BAHA station also registered an elevation in <italic>mcr-1</italic>, <italic>blaOXA-48</italic>, <italic>blaSHV</italic>, and <italic>blaVIM</italic> genes within the effluents.</p><p id="Par50">Regarding the eradication of resistance-spreading species, TIZNIT demonstrated a remarkable 99% reduction in both periods, while DRARGA achieved the same percentage in February. Additionally, the AOURIR station exhibited a 96% reduction in July. Conversely, the ANZA station recorded the lowest elimination percentages, with 26% in February and 53% in July. Regarding the final element of the microbiome, M&#x02019;ZAR achieved the highest elimination percentage in February, reaching 62%. However, certain stations exhibited limited effectiveness in microbiome elimination, leading to an effluent increase. This was particularly noticeable at AOURIR and AIT BAHA in February, as well as TIZNIT and DRARGA in July. In summary, it can be asserted that the efficacy of a treatment is contingent upon numerous variables, encompassing influent quality, composition, physicochemical parameters, seasonal variations, and potentially undisclosed factors.</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec13" sec-type="discussion"><title>Discussion</title><p id="Par51">This study provides a spatio-temporal analysis of the resistome and microbiome in wastewater treatment plants in Agadir, Morocco, using a metagenomic approach. Six facilities with varying treatment processes were sampled during February and July. The results show that the predominant resistance genes are related to aminoglycosides, LMS, macrolides, and lincosamides. Twenty-one species with the potential to disseminate resistance throughout the environment and potentially transfer it to humans have been identified. Out of these, 18 species have been observed. The species predominantly detected across all samples include <italic>Streptococcus salivarius</italic>,<italic> Escherichia coli</italic>,<italic> Klebsiella pneumoniae</italic>, and <italic>Citrobacter freundii.</italic></p><p id="Par52">The 16&#x000a0;S primer pool enables the characterization of the microbiome with a resolution that extends to the species level. Predominantly featured among species are <italic>Acetobacterium sp</italic>, <italic>Achromobacter sp</italic>, <italic>Acidovorax sp</italic>, and <italic>Acinetobacter sp</italic>.</p><p id="Par53">The effectiveness of wastewater treatment depends on various factors, including the quality and composition of the influent, physicochemical parameters, seasonal variations, and potentially other undisclosed variables. In our study, the most efficient WWTPs were Aourir, which employs an activated sludge process with aerobic treatment, and Tiznit with a lagooning system. Multiple studies have investigated different treatment strategies commonly used in WWTPs for their efficiency in removing antibiotic resistance genes (ARGs) and antibiotic-resistant bacteria.</p><p id="Par54">For instance, aerobic and anaerobic-aerobic systems generally demonstrate better ARG removal compared to anaerobic-only systems<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR34">34</xref></sup>. The removal efficiency of these systems varies between 33% and 85%, though some studies noted an increase in the relative abundance of certain ARGs<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR35">35</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR37">37</xref></sup>. To achieve higher removal rates, up to 99%, membrane bioreactors<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR38">38</xref></sup> and advanced disinfection methods like chlorination, ozonation, and UV irradiation have proven effective<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR39">39</xref></sup>, though some of these methods can be costly. Constructed wetlands and hybrid systems have also shown promising results in removing ARGs and resistant bacteria<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR40">40</xref></sup>.</p><p id="Par55">For comprehensive reviews on the efficacy of these treatment methods, please refer to Uluseker et al.<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR41">41</xref></sup> and Huang et al.<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR42">42</xref></sup></p><p id="Par56">The emergence of antibiotic resistance poses a significant global public health challenge, as bacterial infections are a leading cause of mortality. Currently, there were around 4.95&#x000a0;million (3.62&#x02013;6.57) deaths associated with bacterial resistance in 2019, of which 1.27&#x000a0;million (95% UI 0.911&#x02013;1.71) were attributed to resistant bacteria<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR43">43</xref></sup>. This resistance can lead to therapeutic impasse. Several healthcare establishments have recorded an increase in nosocomial infections, mainly due to bacteria producing extended-spectrum B-lactamases (ESBL)<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR44">44</xref></sup>. The epidemiology of this phenomenon shows a worrying growth, especially with the excessive use of antibiotics in various fields, such as the treatment of infections in humans or animal husbandry. This phenomenon is exacerbated by the existence of several means of dissemination, notably conjugative plasmids, transposable elements and integrons, which enable the genes conferring resistance to be moved between different bacteria<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR45">45</xref></sup>. In addition, repeated administration of antibiotics and non-compliance with dosages encourage selection pressure and cause the acquisition and dissemination of drug-resistant strains. The World Health Organization (WHO) considers infections caused by these bacteria to be a priority for treatment, due to their potential impact on public health. It can even affect food security and influence the development of countries. This phenomenon is currently affecting several countries to varying degrees, especially in the wake of the COVID-19 pandemic. Epidemiological monitoring of this resistance is an important means of limiting the spread of resistant bacteria and implementing mitigation strategies. Such biomonitoring requires global action to establish a standardized method for comparing data from different countries<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR46">46</xref></sup>. This approach enhances the analysis and communication of results, enabling informed decision-making. Biomonitoring underscores the importance of proper antibiotic use in both human and animal health, adherence to hygiene practices, and the development of strategies to maintain antibiotic efficacy.</p><p id="Par57">Canonical Correspondence Analysis (CCA) was used to explore how various physicochemical parameters relate to the presence of antibiotic resistance genes (ARGs). Factors like pH, Temperature, Dissolved O<sub>2</sub>, Conductivity, Salinity, Turbidity, COD, Calcium, TH, Magnesium, TAC, Chlorides, and AnSb were studied as independent variables, while the abundance of ARG was the dependent variable. Results indicated that these physicochemical parameters collectively explain a significant portion of the variation in ARG composition (CCA1: <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.007), suggesting a meaningful link between the overall physicochemical environment and antibiotic resistance gene distribution. While CCA1 showed significance, subsequent axes (CCA2 to CCA13) didn&#x02019;t display statistically significant connections with ARG composition. These findings highlight the role of specific physicochemical parameters in shaping ARG profiles and emphasize the need for further research into the individual parameters that drive this relationship. It is important to note that although CCA identifies associations, more research is needed to understand the underlying mechanisms and potential causal factors behind these observed relationships. For the relationship between other resistance genes and physicochemical parameters using Canonical Correspondence Analysis, the research revealed that these parameters accounted for a substantial portion (about 85.6%) of the observed variability in ORG prevalence. Notably, CCA1 showed significant correlation (<italic>p</italic>&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.05) with the physicochemical factors, underlining their role in influencing ORG composition. Eigenvalue analysis demonstrated that CCA1 was a key contributor, explaining approximately 39.85% of the total variance. These findings shed light on the intricate connections between ORGs and environmental conditions. The association between antibiotic resistance genes (ARGs) and mobile genetic elements (MGEs), particularly integrase and the species accountable for resistance dissemination, demonstrated robust correlations (Spearman correlation of 0.82 and 0.87, respectively) (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig7" ref-type="fig">7</xref>).</p><p id="Par58">
<fig id="Fig7"><label>Fig. 7</label><caption><p>Spearman correlation analysis among Mobile Genetic Elements (MGE), Antibiotic Resistance Genes (ARG), microbiome and species.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_76773_Fig7_HTML" id="d33e2206"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par59">In contrast, the correlation with the microbiome exhibited a lesser significance at a value of 0.46. Similarly, a robust correlation of 0.81 was observed between MGEs and the species responsible for disseminating resistance. The figure below provides a detailed representation of the co-occurrence between the resistome and microbiome (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig8" ref-type="fig">8</xref>).</p><p id="Par60">
<fig id="Fig8"><label>Fig. 8</label><caption><p>Resistome and Microbiome co-occurrence network analysis.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_76773_Fig8_HTML" id="d33e2221"/></fig>
</p></sec><sec id="Sec14" sec-type="conclusion"><title>Conclusion</title><p id="Par61">Water scarcity is an urgent global challenge. The reuse of wastewater poses numerous hazards despite offering a potential solution. The analysis of the resistome and microbiome within wastewater treatment plants revealed the emergence of numerous genes, notably <italic>mph(E)</italic>,<italic> erm(B)</italic>, <italic>aadS</italic>, <italic>strB/aph6</italic>, <italic>blaSHV</italic>, and <italic>blaCTX</italic>. Pathogens identified include <italic>Escherichia coli</italic>, <italic>Streptococcus salivarius</italic>, <italic>Klebsiella pneumoniae</italic>, <italic>Arcobacter cryaerophilus</italic>, Clostridium species, and Acinetobacter species. High levels of mobile genetic elements were also observed. The coexistence of MGE and antimicrobial resistance genes underscores their pivotal role in facilitating the acquisition and dissemination of ARGs within microbial communities through horizontal gene transfer. Several purification methods have demonstrated effectiveness in removing a significant portion of these genes and their associated pathogens. However, additional treatment is necessary to completely eliminate these contaminants, ensuring that the water can be safely utilized without posing health risks.</p></sec></body><back><fn-group><fn><p><bold>Publisher&#x02019;s note</bold></p><p>Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.</p></fn></fn-group><notes notes-type="author-contribution"><title>Author contributions</title><p>Maryem Wardi : Conceptualization, Methodology, Formal analysis, Writing - original draft, Writing - review &#x00026; editing. Zohra Lemkhente : Conceptualization, Methodology, Validation, Formal analysis, Writing - original draft, Writing - review &#x00026; editing. Aicha Ait Alla : Conceptualization, Methodology, Resources. Noureddine Slimani : Conceptualization, Resources. M&#x02019;hamed Abali : Conceptualization, Resources. Youssef Idaghdour : Conceptualization, Methodology, Formal analysis, review &#x00026; editing. Ahmed Belmouden : Supervision, Conceptualization, Resources, Writing - original draft, Writing - review &#x00026; editing.</p></notes><notes notes-type="funding-information"><title>Funding</title><p>This work has benefit from funding of the National Center for Scientific and Technical Research (CNRST): GRANT_NUMBER: Cov/2020/33, &#x0201c;Host-Pathogen Interaction in COVID-19 /SARS-CoV-2: Clinical and Genetic Study&#x0201d;.</p></notes><notes notes-type="data-availability"><title>Data availability</title><p>The datasets generated during and/or analysed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.</p></notes><notes><title>Declarations</title><notes id="FPar1" notes-type="COI-statement"><title>Competing interests</title><p id="Par62">The authors declare no competing interests.</p></notes><notes id="FPar2"><title>Ethical approval</title><p id="Par63">Not applicable.</p></notes></notes><ref-list id="Bib1"><title>References</title><ref id="CR1"><label>1.</label><citation-alternatives><element-citation id="ec-CR1" publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Dolan</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Evaluating the economic impact of water scarcity in a changing world</article-title><source>Nat. Commun.</source><year>2021</year><volume>12</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>10</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/s41467-021-22194-0</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">33397941</pub-id>
